course: dictionary
lesson: 5049
title: Formal words on signs and in reports
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like they belong in an official letter or on a notice. Inoperative is one of them. It is a formal word for something that is not working or not in use. You are more likely to see it on a sign or in a report than in a chat with friends.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «This machine is inoperative» tells you the machine is broken or switched off, because a machine that is working is not described that way. Ask yourself: what would a person say in ordinary speech? They would say broken, out of order or not working. Those are your easy swaps.
explain: Safety notices use this word because it is clear and exact. «The smoke alarm is inoperative» is a warning: an alarm that cannot sound will not warn you of a fire, so it must be fixed at once. When you see a formal word, look for the everyday words nearby, and check that your meaning still fits the sentence.
fact: Inoperative means not working or not in use.
fact: It is a formal word, so you will often see it on signs, in reports and in rules.
fact: Everyday words with the same meaning are broken, out of order and not working.
fact: A rule or law that no longer has any effect can also be called inoperative.
fact: In US politics, an «inoperative statement» is an older statement that is now admitted to be false.
exercise: «This machine is inoperative.» What does inoperative mean? | *Not working or switched off | Very noisy | Painted bright red | Working very fast
exercise: Dad says the smoke alarm is inoperative, so he must fix it. Why? | It makes a lovely sound | It is working perfectly | *It is not working, and it is dangerous | It is a brand-new alarm
exercise: Which everyday phrase means the same as inoperative? | Out of bed | *Out of order | Out of tune | Out of breath
test: «The old rule is inoperative now, so nobody has to follow it.» What does inoperative mean here? | *Without any effect, so it no longer applies | Very strong and hard to break | Written in gold letters | Used by everyone every day
test: Which word is the most formal, the kind you would find on an official sign? | *inoperative | broken | not working | out of order
exam: Officials once called a false statement an «inoperative statement»; what does this long word tell you about the statement? | *It was false | It was true and very important | It was read aloud at a party | It was very long
